/// \defgroup grp_w1012_dirtysock_crypto_comm DirtySock RSA arithmetic, CommTCP and NetGameUtil control
/// \ingroup grp_network
/// \brief The big-number arithmetic behind DirtySock's RSA operation, two
///        small functions of DirtySock's TCP comm transport, and
///        NetGameUtil's control call that sets the packet width and queue
///        depths its transports are built with.
///
/// DirtySock is EA's networking library. BFME 2 uses it under the online
/// services (\ref sub_network), and FESL's LAN theater emulator also reaches
/// it when it advertises a LAN game. The Zero Hour source release contains
/// no DirtySock code. This group covers three of its source files:
/// - **cryptrsa.c** holds the private helpers of DirtySDK's CryptRSA module:
///   conversion between big-endian byte strings and arrays of 16-bit words,
///   addition, subtraction, a bit test, modular multiplication and modular
///   exponentiation. They take their bodies and names from an EA DirtySDK
///   source release (the donor package identifies itself as DirtySDK
///   7.5.3); BFME 2's own DirtySDK release is not established. The file's
///   one external entry point, which runs _Exponentiate in place on a
///   caller-supplied RSA state buffer, is not documented here.
/// - **commtcp.cpp** holds CommTCPUnlisten and CommTCPResolve, two
///   functions of the comm transport whose creation routine gives it the
///   name "TCP". CommTCPResolve's name is in the message it logs.
///   CommTCPUnlisten logs nothing. Its name is the ledger's, carried over
///   from the BFME 1 reconstruction without cited evidence, and BFME 2
///   evidence makes it doubtful (see its entry).
/// - **netgameutil.cpp** holds NetGameUtilControl, which sets the values
///   that NetGameUtil's connection setup passes to every comm transport it
///   creates, the TCP transport among them.
///
/// The SHA-1 module that sits next to cryptrsa.c, CryptSha1, is documented
/// with MD5 in \ref grp_w2_wwlib_md5_sha1.
///
/// How the RSA code is reached, from retail call sites. The callers are
/// described here by role only:
/// - ProtoSSL's certificate check. It hashes the signed part of a
///   certificate with MD5 or SHA-1, looks the issuer up in a CA table
///   built into the executable, loads that CA's modulus and public exponent
///   into an RSA state, runs the RSA operation on the certificate's
///   signature and compares the trailing digest-sized bytes of the result
///   with the hash. The built-in table in retail holds three CAs: EA's
///   "OTG3 Certificate Authority" with public exponent 3 and a 1024-bit
///   modulus, RSA Data Security's "Secure Server Certification Authority"
///   with exponent 65537 and a 125-byte modulus, and EA's "OTG Certificate
///   Authority" with exponent 65537 and a 1024-bit modulus. Both exponents
///   take _Exponentiate's fast paths, and the odd-length modulus exercises
///   the odd-length handling in _ToWords and _FromWords.
/// - DirtySock's secure-connection code. It runs the same entry on a block
///   that it then sends to the peer, which fits encrypting key material
///   under the server's public key. That purpose is inferred, not
///   established.
///
/// How the transport code is reached, again described by role:
/// - NetGameUtil's creation routine allocates a zeroed state and calls
///   NetGameUtilControl three times to set its defaults.
/// - NetGameUtil's connection setup builds up to four comm transports,
///   chosen by flag bits in its connection parameters, and creates each one
///   with the stored width and the 'minp' and 'mout' counts. One of them is
///   the TCP transport.
/// - The TCP transport's creation routine stores its operations in a table
///   of function pointers in the transport reference. CommTCPResolve is
///   stored in that table, and no direct call to it has been traced.
///   CommTCPUnlisten is not in the table; it is called by the transport's
///   destroy routine, which is itself one of the table's operations.
///
/// No traced caller of anything in this group is part of the lockstep game
/// logic.
///
/// \par Modding notes
/// - Nothing here is data-driven. No INI field, map script, Lua call or
///   string table entry reaches these functions. The CA keys, the
///   exponents that get a fast path, the 4,096-bit size ceiling of the RSA
///   arithmetic, the NetGameUtil defaults and the 512 cap on 'mwid' are all
///   compiled in.
/// - Changing them cannot desynchronise a game or break replays or saves,
///   because none of it runs in the game logic. It can break the online
///   connection: a certificate whose issuer is not one of the built-in
///   CAs, or whose signature does not verify against that CA's key, fails
///   ProtoSSL's certificate check.
/// - The 4,096-bit ceiling belongs to the arithmetic helpers only. The RSA
///   state that the entry point and its setup helper use has room for a
///   modulus of only a little over 1024 bits. The setup helper stores the
///   modulus and exponent lengths first and then copies the modulus without
///   a length check, so a longer modulus overwrites the exponent length and
///   the operation goes wrong. The ProtoSSL routine that parses and checks
///   the certificate also rejects, while parsing, a signature longer than
///   1024 bits. Replacing a built-in CA with a larger key, such as any
///   current 2048-bit CA, therefore needs the state layout, the setup code
///   and that routine changed as well, not just the table entry.
///
/// \par Remastering notes
/// - The RSA helpers are portable C. They convert to and from big-endian
///   bytes explicitly, so the host byte order does not matter, and their
///   integer types are TU-local typedefs of int, unsigned int, unsigned
///   short and unsigned char, which keep their widths on the usual 32-bit
///   and 64-bit ABIs.
/// - The arithmetic is a minimal public-key implementation. The exponent is
///   folded into 32 bits, so it can only verify and encrypt with small
///   public exponents, never apply a private key. Multiplication is
///   bit-serial and the timing depends on the data. A port that replaces
///   ProtoSSL with a platform TLS library removes both traced users; nothing
///   else needs this code.
/// - The certificate check compares only the trailing digest bytes of the
///   RSA result and does not check the padding in front of them. Combined
///   with a public exponent of 3, that is the pattern behind well-known
///   signature-forgery attacks, so a remaster that keeps online play should
///   use a maintained TLS stack rather than this code.
/// - NetGameUtil's packet width and queue depths, and the TCP transport's
///   buffers sized from them, are fixed numbers set at creation, not
///   configuration. Which online features open NetGameUtil connections has
///   not been traced.

/// \file DirtySock/cryptrsa.c
/// \ingroup grp_w1012_dirtysock_crypto_comm
/// \brief DirtySDK's RSA arithmetic: byte and word conversion, multi-word
///        add, subtract and bit test, modular multiplication and modular
///        exponentiation.
///
/// Numbers are arrays of 16-bit words with the most significant word
/// first. Arithmetic runs in 32-bit accumulators, so a carry or borrow is
/// whatever spills above bit 15. The working arrays are fixed-size stack
/// buffers of 256 words, which caps a modulus at 4,096 bits. Nothing checks
/// that limit. The RSA state that the entry point reads holds a much smaller
/// modulus; see the Modding notes of \ref grp_w1012_dirtysock_crypto_comm.
///
/// All seven helpers have static linkage, as in the donor. In retail the
/// file's one external entry calls _Exponentiate, _Exponentiate calls
/// _ToWords, _Multiply and _FromWords, and _Multiply calls _Addition,
/// _Subtract and _BitTest; no other caller of any helper has been traced.
/// The entry is placed in this file so that the helpers can keep their
/// static linkage.
///
/// The donor's general-case exponentiation logs a warning that it is
/// likely to be slow. Its logging macro compiles to nothing in this build,
/// and the warning text does not appear in retail.

/// \fn static int32_t _Addition(uint16_t *pResult, int32_t iWidth, uint16_t *pAdd1, uint16_t *pAdd2)
/// \brief Adds two multi-word numbers of the same width and returns the
///        carry out of the most significant word.
///
/// The sum is formed from the least significant word upwards, carrying in a
/// 32-bit accumulator. _Multiply uses it to double its running value and to
/// add the multiplicand, and uses the returned carry to decide whether the
/// modulus must be subtracted.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// Each result word is written after both input words at that position have
/// been read, so pResult may be the same array as either input. _Multiply
/// relies on this when it doubles a value by adding it to itself.
///
/// \param[out] pResult Receives iWidth words, most significant first.
/// \param iWidth The number of 16-bit words in each operand.
/// \param pAdd1 The first addend, iWidth words.
/// \param pAdd2 The second addend, iWidth words.
/// \return 1 if the sum overflowed iWidth words, otherwise 0.
/// \see _Subtract, _Multiply

/// \fn static int32_t _Subtract(uint16_t *pResult, int32_t iWidth, uint16_t *pSub1, uint16_t *pSub2)
/// \brief Subtracts one multi-word number from another of the same width
///        and returns the borrow out of the most significant word.
///
/// _Multiply calls it to compute its running value minus the modulus into
/// a second buffer. A zero return means the value was at least the modulus,
/// so the reduced copy is the one to keep.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// The difference is formed from the least significant word upwards. Each
/// step subtracts the previous borrow from the word difference in 32-bit
/// unsigned arithmetic; the low 16 bits are the result word and bit 16 is
/// the next borrow. As in _Addition, pResult may be the same array as
/// either operand.
///
/// \param[out] pResult Receives pSub1 minus pSub2, modulo 2 to the power
///        16 times iWidth.
/// \param iWidth The number of 16-bit words in each operand.
/// \param pSub1 The minuend.
/// \param pSub2 The subtrahend.
/// \return 1 if pSub2 was greater than pSub1, otherwise 0.
/// \see _Addition, _Multiply

/// \fn static int32_t _BitTest(uint16_t *pValue, int32_t iWidth, int32_t iBit)
/// \brief Tests one bit of a multi-word number, counting bit 0 as the least
///        significant bit of the last word.
///
/// _Multiply calls it once per bit of the multiplier, from the top bit
/// down, to decide whether to add the multiplicand at that step.
///
/// \param pValue The number, iWidth words, most significant first.
/// \param iWidth The number of 16-bit words in pValue.
/// \param iBit The bit number, from 0 to 16 times iWidth minus 1. It is not
///        range-checked.
/// \return Nonzero if the bit is set. The value is the bit's weight within
///         its word, not 1, so callers should only test it against zero.
/// \see _Multiply

/// \fn static void _Multiply(uint16_t *pResult, int32_t iWidth, uint16_t *pMul1, uint16_t *pMul2, uint16_t *pMod)
/// \brief Computes pMul1 times pMul2 modulo pMod, all as iWidth-word
///        numbers.
///
/// This is the only multiplication the RSA code has. _Exponentiate calls it
/// for every squaring and every multiplication of the power ladder.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// - Binary double-and-add, from the top bit of pMul1 down. It runs once for
///   every bit of the width (16 times iWidth steps), whatever the operands'
///   values.
/// - At each step the running value is doubled with _Addition. _Subtract
///   then computes the value minus the modulus into a second buffer. If
///   that did not borrow, or the doubling carried out of the top word, the
///   two buffers swap roles, which keeps the reduced copy without copying
///   it.
/// - If _BitTest finds the current bit of pMul1 set, pMul2 is added and the
///   same conditional reduction follows.
/// - One conditional subtraction per step is enough only while the running
///   value and pMul2 stay below the modulus. The function does not reduce
///   pMul2 first, so callers must pass a reduced multiplicand.
/// - The two working buffers are 256-word stack arrays. The result is
///   copied to pResult only at the end, so pResult may be the same array as
///   pMul1 or pMul2; _Exponentiate squares in place this way.
///
/// \param[out] pResult Receives the product modulo pMod, iWidth words.
/// \param iWidth The number of 16-bit words in every operand. At most 256;
///        not checked.
/// \param pMul1 The multiplier. Only its bits are examined.
/// \param pMul2 The multiplicand. Must be less than pMod.
/// \param pMod The modulus.
/// \see _Exponentiate, _Addition, _Subtract, _BitTest

/// \fn static int32_t _ToWords(uint16_t *pResult, int32_t iWidth, const uint8_t *pSource, int32_t iLength)
/// \brief Converts a big-endian byte string into the 16-bit word form the
///        arithmetic uses.
///
/// _Exponentiate converts the modulus and the base with it and takes the
/// modulus's word count as the working width.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// - The number of words needed is the byte count divided by two, rounded
///   up. If iWidth is larger, zero words pad the front.
/// - If the byte count is odd, the first byte becomes a word on its own and
///   the remaining bytes pair up. A 125-byte modulus, such as one of the
///   built-in CA keys, therefore becomes 63 words.
/// - A non-negative iWidth smaller than the word count converts only the
///   leading bytes. Its only caller, _Exponentiate, always passes -1.
///
/// \param[out] pResult Receives the words, most significant first.
/// \param iWidth The width to produce, in words, or a negative value to
///        produce exactly as many words as the bytes need.
/// \param pSource The bytes, most significant first.
/// \param iLength The number of bytes.
/// \return The number of words the bytes need, whatever iWidth was.
/// \see _FromWords, _Exponentiate

/// \fn static void _FromWords(const uint16_t *pSource, int32_t iWidth, uint8_t *pResult, int32_t iLength)
/// \brief Writes the low-order words of a multi-word number out as
///        big-endian bytes.
///
/// _Exponentiate calls it to produce its result in the modulus's byte
/// length.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// iLength is halved, rounding down, to get a word count. That many words
/// from the end of the number are written, two bytes each, most
/// significant byte first.
///
/// \note With an odd iLength, the value's iLength minus 1 low-order bytes
///       are written to the first iLength minus 1 positions of pResult. The
///       value's most significant byte is dropped, and the final byte of
///       the buffer is left unchanged. For the odd-length CA key the dropped
///       byte is the leading byte of the RSA result. The RSA wrapper the
///       certificate check calls allows for this: it reads the digest from
///       the bytes that end at its input length rounded down to an even
///       number.
/// \param pSource The number, iWidth words, most significant first.
/// \param iWidth The number of words in pSource.
/// \param[out] pResult Receives the bytes.
/// \param iLength The byte length wanted.
/// \see _ToWords, _Exponentiate

/// \fn static void _Exponentiate(uint8_t *pResult, uint8_t *pPowerof, const uint8_t *pModulus, int32_t iModSize, const uint8_t *pExponent, int32_t iExpSize)
/// \brief Computes pPowerof raised to pExponent modulo pModulus: the RSA
///        operation for a public key.
///
/// This is the whole RSA computation. The file's entry point calls it with
/// the operand buffer as both pPowerof and pResult and with the modulus and
/// exponent stored in the same RSA state, so the result replaces the input.
/// ProtoSSL's certificate check uses it to recover the digest from a
/// certificate signature, and the secure-connection code uses it on a block
/// it sends to the peer.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// - The modulus and the base are converted with _ToWords. The base is read
///   as iModSize bytes, the same length as the modulus. The modulus's word
///   count becomes the working width.
/// - The exponent bytes are folded big-endian into a 32-bit value. Only the
///   low 32 bits of a longer exponent survive, which is why this function
///   cannot apply a private key.
/// - Three common public exponents have fixed squaring chains: 3 takes two
///   calls to _Multiply, 17 takes five and 65537 takes seventeen.
/// - Any other exponent uses right-to-left square-and-multiply, starting
///   from 1: for each exponent bit, lowest first, the accumulator is
///   multiplied by the base when the bit is set, and the base is squared.
/// - The result is written with _FromWords in iModSize bytes, subject to
///   _FromWords's odd-length behaviour.
/// - Three 256-word stack arrays hold the modulus, base and accumulator.
///
/// \param[out] pResult Receives the result, iModSize bytes, big-endian.
///        It may be the same buffer as pPowerof.
/// \param pPowerof The base, iModSize bytes, big-endian. Expected to be less
///        than the modulus; this is not checked.
/// \param pModulus The modulus, big-endian.
/// \param iModSize The modulus length in bytes, at most 512 for the working
///        arrays. The RSA state the entry point reads it from has room for
///        far less; see the group's Modding notes.
/// \param pExponent The public exponent, big-endian.
/// \param iExpSize The exponent length in bytes. Values above 4 lose the
///        exponent's high bytes.
/// \par Remastering
/// Any standard big-number or crypto library computes the same value. If a
/// port keeps ProtoSSL, the result must stay byte-identical, including the
/// odd-length truncation described under _FromWords, because the
/// certificate check locates the digest from that layout.
/// \see _Multiply, _ToWords, _FromWords

/// \file DirtySock/commtcp.cpp
/// \ingroup grp_w1012_dirtysock_crypto_comm
/// \brief Two functions of DirtySock's TCP comm transport: CommTCPUnlisten
///        and CommTCPResolve.
///
/// A DirtySock comm transport is a reference structure that carries its own
/// table of operation pointers, filled by the transport's creation routine,
/// so callers drive every transport the same way. NetGameUtil's connection
/// setup creates the TCP transport when its connection flags ask for it.
/// Neither function's full parameter list is known: both use the C calling
/// convention, whose decorated names do not record parameters, and the
/// declarations here are only as wide as each body needs.

/// \fn int CommTCPUnlisten(CommTCPRef *ref)
/// \brief Moves a TCP transport out of the one state its destroy routine
///        guards on, into the next state; in any other state it does
///        nothing.
///
/// Its only traced caller is the TCP transport's destroy routine. That
/// routine calls it when the transport is in the same state this function
/// tests for. Then, among other teardown, it closes the transport's socket
/// if it has one, resets its lock, frees its two buffers and frees the
/// reference.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// In the matching state it clears one field of the reference, a field the
/// creation routine also zeroes, and advances the state. It closes nothing
/// itself. The TCP transport's status operation reports the tested state as
/// a status of its own, and reports the state this function moves to the
/// same way as a newly created transport. What the states mean is not
/// established.
///
/// \note The name is the ledger's, carried over from the BFME 1
/// reconstruction, which cites no evidence for it. BFME 2 evidence makes it
/// doubtful:
/// - The UDP transport's operation table holds CommUDPResolve, CommUdpListen
///   and CommUDPConnect, whose names appear in their own log messages, at
///   fixed positions. By that order, the position between listen and
///   connect is where an unlisten operation would sit. The TCP transport's
///   table holds a different function there, and this function is not in
///   the table at all.
/// - This body has the shape of the UDP transport's close step: it tests
///   the same state, clears the field at the same position in the reference
///   and sets the same next state. The UDP step also sends a close message
///   first. The UDP destroy routine guards its call to that step in the
///   same way.
///
/// \param[in,out] ref The TCP transport reference.
/// \return Always 0, including when the state did not match.
/// \see CommTCPResolve, NetGameUtilControl

/// \fn int CommTCPResolve()
/// \brief The TCP transport's resolve operation, which is not supported: it
///        logs that and fails.
///
/// The transport's creation routine stores it in the reference's operation
/// table; no direct call to it has been traced. The body ignores whatever
/// arguments the caller passes. Its UDP sibling CommUDPResolve does the
/// same, and is declared with the reference, an address string, an output
/// buffer, its length and a divider character, which suggests what callers
/// pass here too.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// It prints "CommTCPResolve: Resolve functionality not supported by
/// CommTCP" (the retail string) through DirtySock's formatted debug print,
/// which hands the text to a print hook, and returns -1.
///
/// \return Always -1.
/// \see CommUDPResolve, CommTCPUnlisten

/// \file DirtySock/netgameutil.cpp
/// \ingroup grp_w1012_dirtysock_crypto_comm
/// \brief NetGameUtilControl, the settings call of DirtySock's NetGameUtil
///        connection helper.
///
/// NetGameUtil sets up a connection over one or more DirtySock comm
/// transports. Its state record holds, among other things, the four values
/// this control call sets. As in commtcp.cpp, the parameter list is the one
/// the body needs; the C calling convention does not record it.

/// \fn void NetGameUtilControl(NetGameUtilRef *ref, int selector, int value)
/// \brief Sets one NetGameUtil setting, chosen by a four-character code,
///        and logs the new value.
///
/// NetGameUtil takes its settings through this call, keyed by
/// four-character codes written in C as multi-character constants such as
/// 'mwid'. NetGameUtil's creation routine calls this function to set its
/// defaults: 'mwid' 240, 'minp' 32 and 'mout' 32. It does not set 'advf',
/// which therefore starts at zero. When the connection setup later creates
/// a comm transport, it passes the stored width and the 'minp' and 'mout'
/// counts to the transport's creation routine. The TCP transport sizes each
/// packet record from the width plus a small header of its own, and sizes
/// its two packet buffers from the two counts.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// - 'mwid': a value up to 512 is stored with 14 added, and the stored value
///   is logged. A larger value is rejected with "'mwid' value of %d is too
///   large" and the old value is kept. There is no lower bound. Why 14 is
///   added is not established.
/// - 'minp', 'mout' and 'advf' are stored unchanged and logged.
/// - Any other code is ignored without a message, and nothing is returned.
/// - Every message goes through DirtySock's formatted debug print, with the
///   prefix "NetGameUtilControl:".
///
/// The code names suggest a maximum packet width, input and output queue
/// depths and an advertising frequency. How the TCP transport uses the
/// first three confirms that the width is a packet payload size and that
/// 'minp' and 'mout' are packet counts; which of its two buffers serves
/// input and which output is not established.
///
/// NetGameUtil's advertising routine, which FESL's LAN theater emulator
/// calls when it advertises a LAN game, passes the stored 'advf' value as
/// the last argument of the advertisement-announce routine. That routine
/// substitutes a default for zero, clamps the value to a small range and
/// writes it into the header of each new advertisement record it creates
/// and sends. This supports the advertising-frequency reading; the exact
/// unit is not established. Because 'advf' starts at zero, the default
/// applies unless something sets it. NetGameUtil's connection setup passes
/// 0 in the same position when it announces through that routine.
///
/// \param[in,out] ref The NetGameUtil state. Must not be null; it is not
///        checked.
/// \param selector The four-character code, packed big-endian into an int.
/// \param value The new value.
/// \par Modding
/// The defaults and the 512 cap are constants in the code, not data.
/// \see CommTCPUnlisten, CommTCPResolve
